<article class="env-warning" id="05D9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05D9">Warning <span data-tag="05D9">4.8.7.10</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be an inner fibration of $\infty $-categories. In the case $n = 1$, our proof of Theorem <a data-tag="05CS" href="/tag/05CS">4.8.7.3</a> shows that $F$ factors as a composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\xrightarrow {F'} \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}0}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})}} \xrightarrow {G} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}, \]
</div>
<p> where $F'$ is full and essentially surjective, and $G$ is a $0$-categorical inner fibration (in particular, $G$ is faithful). Beware that this generally does not coincide with the factorization constructed in Example <a data-tag="05CV" href="/tag/05CV">4.8.7.6</a>. If $u: X \rightarrow Y$ is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ having the property that $F(u)$ is an identity morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, then the functor $F'$ carries $X$ and $Y$ to the same object of $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}0}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})}}$. Consequently, the functor $F'$ is generally not bijective on objects. </p>
<p>A related phenomenon occurs in the case $n = 0$. By construction, $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}-1}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})}}$ is the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ spanned by objects of the form $F(X)$, where $X$ is an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If the inner fibration $F$ is not an isofibration, this subcategory might be smaller than the essential image of $F$. </p>
</article>
